# ==============================================================================
# SETTINGS FOR RUNNING THE APP ON YOUR COMPUTER
# ==============================================================================
#
# What is this file?
#   The app needs to know which Supabase project (your database and logins) to
#   use. Instead of writing that into the code, the app reads it from a file
#   named .env.local that stays on your computer. This file, .env.example, is a
#   template for it, with fake values (XXXX).
#
# How to use it:
#   1. Make a copy of this file in the same folder, named .env.local
#      (in the terminal: cp .env.example .env.local).
#   2. In .env.local, replace each XXXX with your own value. Keep the quotes.
#   3. Save .env.local. If the app is running (npm run dev), stop it with
#      Ctrl+C and start it again: the app reads .env.local when it starts.
#
# How to read it:
#   - Each setting is one line: NAME="value". Don't put spaces around the =.
#   - Lines that start with # are notes for people, like this one. The app
#     ignores them.
#
# Is it safe?
#   - .env.local is never uploaded to GitHub: the project's .gitignore file
#     tells Git to skip it. This template IS on GitHub, so it holds only fake
#     values. Never put your real values in this template.
#   - Both settings below are meant to be public: settings whose names start
#     with NEXT_PUBLIC_ are built into the web pages that every visitor's
#     browser downloads. So never put a secret key in .env.local.
#   - The automated tests need more settings, some of them secret. Those go in
#     a different file: see .env.test.example. You don't need it to run the app.
# ==============================================================================

# Your Supabase project's web address. It looks like
# https://abcdefghijklmnopqrst.supabase.co
# Where to find it: in the Supabase dashboard, open your project. The address
# is under the project's name, with a Copy button.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://XXXX.supabase.co"

# Your Supabase project's publishable key. It starts with sb_publishable_
# Where to find it: Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > API Keys.
# Why "ANON_KEY"? Older Supabase projects called this key the "anon" key, and
# the app's code still uses that name. If your project only has the older keys,
# put its anon key here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="sb_publishable_XXXX"
